409
00:21:42,716 --> 00:21:42,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

410
00:21:43,296 --> 00:21:44,557
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you even speak of it?

411
00:21:45,238 --> 00:21:45,358
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

412
00:21:45,798 --> 00:21:47,380
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless it's another convention.

413
00:21:47,620 --> 00:21:47,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

414
00:21:49,065 --> 00:21:56,388
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when people don't know how to escape this, don't know how to deal with the problem of assumption, so then they blame the designation.

415
00:21:56,728 --> 00:22:00,189
[SPEAKER_00]: They say the conventions, designations, words, names, nāma.

416
00:22:00,689 --> 00:22:02,090
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the problem.

417
00:22:02,110 --> 00:22:06,531
[SPEAKER_00]: So you stop doing it, you get to the real reality that you just named.

418
00:22:06,671 --> 00:22:07,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

419
00:22:07,932 --> 00:22:13,534
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you realize naming is not a matter of choice either, because whenever there is a degree of resistance, there you go.

420
00:22:14,174 --> 00:22:14,834
[SPEAKER_00]: You just named it.

421
00:22:14,854 --> 00:22:16,055
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a degree of resistance.

422
00:22:16,995 --> 00:22:17,715
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not a new.

423
00:22:17,735 --> 00:22:18,475
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I said.

424
00:22:18,735 --> 00:22:21,316
[SPEAKER_00]: The target understands the extent of designation.

425
00:22:21,336 --> 00:22:23,356
[SPEAKER_00]: Designation just means something's been recognized.

426
00:22:23,396 --> 00:22:24,817
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't necessarily mean you've given it.

427
00:22:24,837 --> 00:22:25,437
[SPEAKER_00]: You're doing it.

428
00:22:25,457 --> 00:22:26,557
[SPEAKER_00]: You're labeling it.

429
00:22:27,477 --> 00:22:31,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, labeling, naming come out of it, but it's far down the road.

430
00:22:31,938 --> 00:22:33,919
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, just the recognition that something's there.

431
00:22:34,499 --> 00:22:40,440
[SPEAKER_00]: People don't know how to deal with the problem, so then they try to identify it with labeling and naming, which is easily dismissible.

432
00:22:40,460 --> 00:22:40,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

433
00:22:40,940 --> 00:22:43,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Just refrain from naming, and that's the reality.

434
00:22:43,701 --> 00:22:47,483
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't refrain from thought that's already a designation in itself, that resists you.

435
00:22:49,644 --> 00:22:54,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, you can't refrain from... You can refrain from engaging upon thought in a certain manner.

436
00:22:54,525 --> 00:22:54,746
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

437
00:22:54,986 --> 00:22:57,767
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't refrain from having thought.

438
00:22:58,987 --> 00:23:03,289
[SPEAKER_00]: And those thoughts are already designated to that extent, that they resist you.

439
00:23:03,629 --> 00:23:03,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

440
00:23:04,089 --> 00:23:07,010
[SPEAKER_00]: And they do resist you because, again, they appear to you.

441
00:23:07,131 --> 00:23:07,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

442
00:23:07,551 --> 00:23:07,891
[SPEAKER_00]: For you.

443
00:23:08,011 --> 00:23:08,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

444
00:23:12,137 --> 00:23:15,440
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mindfulness of the body is then mindfulness of your thoughts.

445
00:23:15,460 --> 00:23:15,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

446
00:23:16,040 --> 00:23:27,751
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness of the thoughts properly, as I said, to the... discerning the extent the thought resists you, or it implies fundamental resistance, which is resistance of the senses and the body.

447
00:23:27,771 --> 00:23:31,554
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mindfulness of the thoughts properly, means mindfulness of the body properly.

448
00:23:33,856 --> 00:23:34,577
[SPEAKER_00]: And everything in between.

449
00:23:35,597 --> 00:23:36,919
[SPEAKER_01]: In between being feelings and moods.

450
00:23:36,939 --> 00:23:37,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

451
00:23:38,617 --> 00:23:41,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Because all of that is inconceivable without feelings and moods.

452
00:23:41,780 --> 00:23:42,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

453
00:23:42,341 --> 00:23:43,502
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just inseparable from.

454
00:23:43,622 --> 00:23:48,668
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can you can pick the aspect you want to do, but you'll be developing all them if you're doing it rightly.

455
00:23:48,788 --> 00:23:48,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

456
00:23:49,088 --> 00:23:55,415
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't need to kind of look at the other one and go, you know, you just... No.

457
00:23:56,056 --> 00:23:58,138
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're comparing them, you have none.

458
00:24:05,249 --> 00:24:08,751
[SPEAKER_00]: And also, another thing to mention, it's like, so you say, okay, so this is...

459
00:24:08,771 --> 00:24:10,552
[SPEAKER_00]: I have a thought of this body.

460
00:24:10,592 --> 00:24:22,039
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't help but imply that there is that outside of this thought from which this body has come, so to speak.

461
00:24:22,059 --> 00:24:23,319
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, that's no matter of choice.

462
00:24:23,339 --> 00:24:24,880
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't stop doing that.

463
00:24:25,341 --> 00:24:27,862
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't remove that implication by choice.

464
00:24:29,103 --> 00:24:31,044
[SPEAKER_00]: But you will uproot the implication

465
00:24:32,246 --> 00:24:33,286
[SPEAKER_00]: By including it.

466
00:24:33,546 --> 00:24:34,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

467
00:24:35,147 --> 00:24:35,847
[SPEAKER_01]: It's something else.

468
00:24:35,927 --> 00:24:41,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, exactly, to the extent it assumes external, to that extent, exclude itself.

469
00:24:41,569 --> 00:24:41,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

470
00:24:41,970 --> 00:24:50,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this pertains to this thought, that's within the domain of thoughts, gratuitously, somehow, implies that it's outside of this domain of thoughts.

471
00:24:50,673 --> 00:24:50,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

472
00:24:51,673 --> 00:24:54,074
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, I can conceive anything outside of my senses.

473
00:24:54,094 --> 00:24:54,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

474
00:24:54,814 --> 00:24:56,775
[SPEAKER_00]: And, like, that's, again, it's in the same

475
00:24:57,697 --> 00:25:04,121
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the same nature, same principle, what we described, when you think you're accessing the objects of your senses.

476
00:25:04,181 --> 00:25:05,302
[SPEAKER_00]: All you can access is your body.

477
00:25:08,444 --> 00:25:11,606
[SPEAKER_00]: All you can access is your senses being affected by different things.

478
00:25:12,166 --> 00:25:14,748
[SPEAKER_00]: See, different things means outside of my senses.

479
00:25:14,968 --> 00:25:15,508
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

480
00:25:15,548 --> 00:25:16,569
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the implication.

481
00:25:17,333 --> 00:25:21,914
[SPEAKER_00]: But once you fully understand it, so you're not to go, oh, I must stop, stop thinking this implication.

482
00:25:21,934 --> 00:25:22,614
[SPEAKER_00]: That's impossible.

483
00:25:22,754 --> 00:25:23,615
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't stop thinking it.

484
00:25:24,115 --> 00:25:29,776
[SPEAKER_00]: But, I should include it within the necessary container of the present enduring body, not exclude it as something outside.

485
00:25:29,836 --> 00:25:30,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

486
00:25:30,556 --> 00:25:33,377
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how the assumption aspect of it fades.

487
00:25:33,657 --> 00:25:33,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

488
00:25:33,857 --> 00:25:35,537
[SPEAKER_00]: But thought in itself was never a problem.

489
00:25:35,717 --> 00:25:35,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

490
00:25:35,918 --> 00:25:38,058
[SPEAKER_00]: Even a wrong thought is not a problem in itself.

491
00:25:38,198 --> 00:25:38,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

492
00:25:38,538 --> 00:25:43,039
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is the, the significance that the, that gratuitously carries.

493
00:25:43,259 --> 00:25:45,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Significantly, there is a real reality behind my reality.

494
00:25:46,631 --> 00:25:50,554
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you discern any of that, any scope of that domain, unless you're thinking it?

495
00:25:50,794 --> 00:25:52,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is fine to think it, but it's not fine that

496
00:26:07,323 --> 00:26:10,806
[SPEAKER_00]: When you think it, you think that your thought is second to it.

497
00:26:11,086 --> 00:26:11,266
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

498
00:26:11,306 --> 00:26:13,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, the only way to know it is to think it.

499
00:26:13,288 --> 00:26:15,470
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically it's the order that's different, isn't it?

500
00:26:15,710 --> 00:26:15,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

501
00:26:16,150 --> 00:26:16,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

502
00:26:16,530 --> 00:26:17,331
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point.

503
00:26:18,452 --> 00:26:26,999
[SPEAKER_00]: That perverted order at the most fundamental level of dhamma and māna, māna is the forerunner of all things, pervert the order, māna is the forerunner of all perverted things.

504
00:26:27,619 --> 00:26:29,881
[SPEAKER_00]: Perversion of lust, aversion and distraction.

505
00:26:31,643 --> 00:26:32,103
[SPEAKER_00]: You approve...

506
00:26:32,363 --> 00:26:35,766
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, you know, polluted stream on top of the mountain.

507
00:26:36,747 --> 00:26:36,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

508
00:26:37,388 --> 00:26:41,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how far down you go, it's gonna carry the same pollution.

509
00:26:41,331 --> 00:26:47,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how much, how hard you're trying to purify the water at the lower points, your work is endless.

510
00:26:47,916 --> 00:26:48,096
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

511
00:26:48,317 --> 00:26:50,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not uprooting it at the source.

512
00:26:50,939 --> 00:26:56,703
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you go and find the source, and clear the pollution in the very source, and then you don't have to worry about...

513
00:26:56,723 --> 00:26:59,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter even if it turns into a whole ocean later on.

514
00:26:59,165 --> 00:26:59,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter.

515
00:26:59,625 --> 00:27:02,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you know it cannot be polluted because you have cleaned up the source.

516
00:27:04,732 --> 00:27:06,254
[SPEAKER_00]: And dissociate the mind and the thoughts.

517
00:27:06,474 --> 00:27:09,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the mind is definable by the resistance of the thoughts.

518
00:27:11,098 --> 00:27:12,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Present enduring thought.

519
00:27:12,760 --> 00:27:17,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Present enduring thought of the body, present enduring thought of the feeling, present enduring thought of the mind, the mood.

520
00:27:20,367 --> 00:27:29,436
[SPEAKER_00]: Present enduring thought of impermanence, present enduring thought of suffering, even present enduring thoughts of you can't own any of this.

521
00:27:31,768 --> 00:27:36,711
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't own any of this and the thoughts that I can't own any of this are unownable.

522
00:27:37,431 --> 00:27:43,834
[SPEAKER_00]: If I understand them as unownable, no idea of ownership can be attained in regard to anything inferior to them, anything within them.

523
00:27:50,478 --> 00:27:58,002
[SPEAKER_01]: And so all perceptions, sights, sounds,

524
00:28:00,270 --> 00:28:01,252
[SPEAKER_01]: Smells this, touches?

525
00:28:02,013 --> 00:28:04,237
[SPEAKER_01]: All... that's all secondary.

526
00:28:07,061 --> 00:28:07,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

527
00:28:13,120 --> 00:28:15,602
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's said the five senses meet in the mind.

528
00:28:15,662 --> 00:28:15,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

529
00:28:16,142 --> 00:28:17,063
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not like they...

530
00:28:17,223 --> 00:28:23,647
[SPEAKER_01]: I think many people today tend to think that that's the kind of building blocks and then they come and they meet in the mind.

531
00:28:23,667 --> 00:28:23,787
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

532
00:28:23,807 --> 00:28:27,410
[SPEAKER_01]: They're constructed together and then reality gets constructed by the mind.

533
00:28:27,450 --> 00:28:30,652
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you conceive anything that's pre-reality?

534
00:28:30,832 --> 00:28:31,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

535
00:28:31,693 --> 00:28:37,237
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you even theorize about pre-reality unless you're theorizing it in the real time, here and now, which is your reality?

536
00:28:38,317 --> 00:28:53,530
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, here you are, using the means of this reality, which is the means of resisting thoughts that designate, to describe that which precedes this resistance and designation, and somehow assume the validity of that world that's utterly inaccessible to you.

537
00:28:55,572 --> 00:28:59,255
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you assuming the axis, that's how these things exist.

538
00:28:59,756 --> 00:29:04,000
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why Buddha said that four great elements find the footing in your experience,

539
00:29:04,906 --> 00:29:05,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you conceive them.

540
00:29:05,927 --> 00:29:09,430
[SPEAKER_00]: You give them the being through your misconceiving, literally.

541
00:29:10,491 --> 00:29:11,172
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what they are.

542
00:29:12,253 --> 00:29:13,294
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, they cannot appear.

543
00:29:14,935 --> 00:29:15,796
[SPEAKER_00]: They can only resist.

544
00:29:16,116 --> 00:29:17,337
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's already designated.

545
00:29:17,617 --> 00:29:19,079
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's already the level of the mind.

546
00:29:23,843 --> 00:29:26,946
[SPEAKER_00]: So, present enduring feeling, you can

547
00:29:27,983 --> 00:29:29,664
[SPEAKER_00]: Approach is a present enduring feeling.

548
00:29:29,704 --> 00:29:34,588
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm feeling like this, while I'm doing this, while I'm feeling like this, while I'm talking, feeling like this, while I'm breathing, I'm feeling like this.

549
00:29:34,648 --> 00:29:37,530
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm still feeling the same while we are sitting and discussing this.

550
00:29:39,091 --> 00:29:42,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you can approach a present enduring feeling, it will also be a present enduring thought.

551
00:29:43,954 --> 00:29:44,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

552
00:29:44,795 --> 00:29:46,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it is a thought, isn't it?

553
00:29:47,016 --> 00:29:47,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

554
00:29:49,088 --> 00:29:52,151
[SPEAKER_00]: But obviously, can you have that thought if there isn't present in your feeling?

555
00:29:52,772 --> 00:29:58,918
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you know the present in your feeling if there isn't... if it isn't a thought, if it isn't an image, if it isn't a dhamma?

556
00:29:59,358 --> 00:29:59,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

557
00:30:08,347 --> 00:30:11,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, there's a tendency to think that the feeling is a thought.

558
00:30:13,580 --> 00:30:14,060
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

559
00:30:14,080 --> 00:30:16,922
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're comparing the approaches, you're not doing either.

560
00:30:17,082 --> 00:30:20,845
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the way to avoid that is to include the feeling within the thought.

561
00:30:20,865 --> 00:30:21,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

562
00:30:21,405 --> 00:30:22,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Include your need to compare.

563
00:30:23,146 --> 00:30:23,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

564
00:30:23,987 --> 00:30:29,830
[SPEAKER_00]: With the present enduring feeling or within any present enduring tear, within any present enduring anger.

565
00:30:31,912 --> 00:30:33,273
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can think about the thoughts.

566
00:30:34,305 --> 00:30:36,447
[SPEAKER_00]: On the basis of being mindful of the present enduring body.

567
00:30:36,647 --> 00:30:36,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

568
00:30:37,587 --> 00:30:41,190
[SPEAKER_00]: You can think about feeling on the basis of present enduring thoughts.

569
00:30:41,570 --> 00:30:45,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Whichever way you rooted your mindfulness, that's fine.

570
00:30:45,713 --> 00:30:47,635
[SPEAKER_00]: But it will have to be rooted in one of these four things.

571
00:30:49,576 --> 00:30:58,402
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing outside of these tiers that you can think or experience that's not within one of them, which implies all the other ones.

